> In this text: "To determine realistic
> values, we start from the well-documented Intention–Behavior Gap. While pedagogical
> interventions typically produce large gains in climate knowledge (g ≈ 0.77) and
> substantial pro-climate intentions (effect sizes up to d ≈ 0.66), translating these
> intentions into enacted behaviours suffers massive attrition: approximately 50% of
> initial positive intentions fail to materialise (Webb & Sheeran, 2006; Sheeran & Webb,
> 2016). The most recent meta-analysis on climate change education reports an average
> effect size of g ≈ 0.36 for self-reported behavioural outcomes (Aeschbach et al., 2025)."
> 
> What are g and d?
**assistant**:
> **$d$** and **$g$** are standardized effect sizes: they express an intervention’s impact in units of standard deviations, making results comparable across studies.
> 
> - **Cohen’s $d$**: typically the difference between treatment and control group means divided by a pooled standard deviation:
>   \[
>   d = \frac{\bar X_{\mathrm{treat}}-\bar X_{\mathrm{control}}}{s_{\mathrm{pooled}}}.
>   \]
> 
> - **Hedges’ $g$**: Cohen’s $d$ with a small-sample bias correction. It is usually slightly smaller than $d$:
>   \[
>   g = Jd,
>   \]
>   where $J$ is a correction factor close to 1.
> 
> So, $g \approx 0.77$ means the intervention increased climate knowledge by roughly **0.77 standard deviations** relative to a comparison condition; $d \approx 0.66$ means intentions were about **0.66 SD** higher.
